    "content": "There are 38 federally recognized Native American tribes headquartered in Oklahoma. The largest, the Cherokee Nation, has over 450,000 citizens. Other major nations with significant, sovereign, and, following the 2020 McGirt v. Oklahoma Supreme Court ruling, reservation lands include the Choctaw, Chickasaw, Muscogee (Creek), and Seminole Nations.Key details regarding major nations in Oklahoma include:Cherokee Nation: Based in Tahlequah, it is the largest tribe in the US, covering 16 counties in northeastern Oklahoma.Choctaw Nation of Oklahoma: Headquartered in Durant, it covers 10.5 counties in southeastern Oklahoma.Chickasaw Nation: Located in south-central Oklahoma with headquarters in Ada.Muscogee Nation: Based in Okmulgee, representing the Muscogee (Creek) people.Seminole Nation of Oklahoma: Located in Seminole County.Osage Nation: Headquartered in Pawhuska.Many other tribes exist throughout the state, including the Citizen Potawatomi Nation, Apache Tribe of Oklahoma, and Delaware Nation. These tribes have a massive economic impact on the state and provide health, education, and social services to their citizens.",
